WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
SQL joins.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:04.400
Choosing a join is never cosmetic.

3
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.033
It decides which rows quietly disappear from your report.

4
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:11.983
A join matches rows from two tables on a key.

5
00:00:11.983 --> 00:00:13.633
There are three main kinds.

6
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:16.800
INNER keeps only the rows that match on both sides.

7
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.133
LEFT keeps everything on the left, and fills the right side with NULLs when there is no match.

8
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:26.100
FULL keeps both sides, and NULL-pads whichever one is missing.

9
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.033
Here is a LEFT JOIN of satellites to their passes.

10
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:33.533
Two satellites matched, so they carry real pass counts.

11
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.054
Aqua had no passes.

12
00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:36.790
LEFT keeps it, with a NULL.

13
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:41.867
An inner join would have dropped exactly the silent satellite ops needs to see.

14
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:45.500
Now the trap that bites everyone in production.

15
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.933
One downlink row, a hundred gigabytes, belongs to station seven.

16
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:54.867
But the station table lists key seven twice, so the join finds two matches.

17
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.600
The downlink now appears twice in the output.

18
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.662
And every sum downstream is silently doubled.

19
00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:04.100
When a total looks about double, suspect the join first.

20
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.367
Here is the satellite query in code.

21
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:12.100
We start from satellites and LEFT JOIN passes, so no satellite is lost.

22
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.867
The date filter on the right table goes inside the ON clause.

23
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:20.833
That way satellites with zero recent passes still show up with a count of zero.

24
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.033
Here is a real version of that trap.

25
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:30.133
The Svalbard ground station was decommissioned and later added again, so its key exists twice.

26
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:32.667
Every pass through it now matches two rows.

27
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:37.267
Total downlinked gigabytes for that station come out exactly doubled.

28
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:39.227
Let's recap.

29
00:01:39.227 --> 00:01:42.267
INNER, LEFT, and FULL decide which rows survive.

30
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:45.833
Duplicate keys multiply rows and inflate your sums.

31
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:48.467
Filters on the right table belong in ON.

32
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:52.614
And a LEFT JOIN plus IS NULL finds rows with no partner.

33
00:01:52.614 --> 00:01:54.400
Next up: window functions.
